PATCH 514: 8f3a took the object from an uninitialised parameter and lost ad3b's AL -- the second
native<->wasm divergence carrier.

FUN_0000_8f3a (asm 0x8f3a-0x8f48) is `mov [di+0x90],al ; movb [di+0xca],3 ; lcall 0f69:7a5b`, reached
ONLY through FUN_0000_a19e's per-object-type dispatch, which passes the object as a HOST POINTER in its
single argument (patch 283).  Patch 394 had taken the object from `param_2` instead -- an uninitialised
stack slot -- and used the low byte of the host pointer as AL.

AL is ad3b's gear-direction selector, which patch 283 explicitly recorded as dropped:
    ad3b: cmpb [di+0x90],1 ; ja 0xad56(ret)
    ad42: cmpw [di+0x34],0xe00 ; jl 0xad57
    ad49: cmpb [di+0x90],1 ; je 0xad56(ret)   ; ad50: mov al,1 ; call 0xa19e
    ad57: cmpb [di+0x90],0 ; je 0xad56(ret)   ; ad5e: mov al,0 ; jmp 0xad52
a19e's dispatch is one-argument, so AL now travels in g_fist_a19e_al -- the mechanism patch 142 uses for
the walk base BP.  0x8f49 (`mov al,1 ; jmp 0x8f3a`) is the same thing as a thunk.

Reading the object from an uninitialised slot is undefined behaviour, so gcc -m32 and emcc disagreed.
Measured on AZER4: the targets are bit-identical through t=292 and differ at t=293 in exactly ONE byte,
DGROUP:0x9004 (3 on native, unchanged 0x68 on wasm) -- and 0x9004 == 0x8f3a + 0xca, i.e. this very store
executed with the garbage `param_2 == 0x8f3a`, the function's own address left on the stack.
